00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.080
My apologies to boiling down
the entire automation thing

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.740
to just automate all the things.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.280
But for the most part,
that's kind of the mentality

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.020
that I like when it comes
to that particular category.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.020
The way that I like
thinking about these things,

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.820
however, is to put
them in buckets.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.820
Because some. of them
overlap, and a lot of them

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.770
are integrated with
some other components.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.190
So let's go ahead and put
these in some buckets.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:55.660
So first of all, I have a
bucket over here for simplicity.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.830
Anything that you're
building, generally speaking,

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.720
should be as simple as possible.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.200
And that's not just
keep it simple, stupid.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.660
But if you find yourself
using a particular tool

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.970
and you're like, wow, I
have to do a lot of work

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.009
in order to get this
tool to work properly,

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:11.590
or there's a lot of glue code
I have to write around it

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.420
or a lot of extra
scripts, chances

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.040
are you might be
doing something that

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.280
