00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.490
that's a big important
thing in and of itself,

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:35.950
so that gets its own bucket.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.740
And then lastly,
bucket three here

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.710
is all about embracing risk,
having SLOs and monitoring.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:44.410
So it's all about establishing
the appropriate SLIs,

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.962
figuring out what your SLOs are
going to be, and making sure

00:02:46.962 --> 00:02:48.670
that you're monitoring
them so you can do

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:51.050
those appropriate calculations.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:53.770
So now that we've got
these types of buckets,

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:55.580
let's approach each
one of these buckets.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.080
And then I'll show you some of
the GCP products and services

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.790
that we have that make it very
easy for you to build, say,

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.570
a planet scale computer
that is properly

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:08.260
monitored, properly metricked,
and is somewhat reliable.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:12.370
But first, there is
actually a catch.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:14.440
Now, what would this
catch actually be?

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.660
Well, it turns out
that when you want

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:18.287
to do things like
hermetic builds

00:03:18.287 --> 00:03:19.870
and if you want to
do things like have

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.030
appropriate monitoring
