00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.030
you need to have an
underlying platform that

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:27.950
is responsive and capable of
running these types of things.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.950
You want something that
has built in automation,

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.050
built in orchestration,
monitoring,

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.620
and so on and so forth.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.370
So what I'm going to
say is the catch here

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:39.640
is that you're going to use
something like the components

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.870
inside of Anthos, or formerly
the Cloud Services Platform,

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.180
but we added a few
things on top of it.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.790
Now, I'm not saying you have
to use that particular product,

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:52.120
just that that product is
actually a meta product that

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.820
actually encompasses a heck
of a lot of other components.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:57.430
For example here, which are--

00:03:57.430 --> 00:03:58.970
I guess they're
showing up there.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.470
You've got a couple
extra components

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.950
you might see here on the
periphery, things like Knative.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.620
You've got Istio and
Kubernetes down there.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:06.670
You've got Cloud Build.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:10.790
And you've got the rest of
the GCP portfolio down here.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:13.700
So let's dive in
with these things.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:17.529
But just to give you
a quick recap of why

00:04:17.529 --> 00:04:20.260
I think this is a really
awesome platform so that we're

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.780
on the same page,
we've got a handful

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.550
of these different
types of components

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:25.780
explained here, right?

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.230
So we've got application-centric
runtime configuration

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.240
orchestration, which is all
of the words in your buzzword

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:32.740
bingo.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:34.870
So go ahead and
check that one off.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:37.120
But in general, it's
basically like you

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.680
need to have a declarative
system that says,

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.270
I want these things to be
running in this fashion, right?

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.030
The core of SRE
to me is all about

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.270
the programmatic operation
of your infrastructure.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:51.588
Your infrastructure
powers your applications.

00:04:51.588 --> 00:04:53.380
Your applications are
tied to your business

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:56.770
goals, which means that you're
programming your business.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:59.020
That's what this is all
about, trying to remove people

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:02.140
from the equation while still
making sure that things run

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.450
properly, and you're reactive
and you're continuing

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:05.930
with your development.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.520
The other thing I like
about Anthos and CSP

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.100
and all of the components
that are inside it are it's

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.650
built from open source
components, which means

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.120
it's hybrid and multi-cloud.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.100
So anything I'm
preaching up here today,

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.450
you can take with you regardless
of where you want to deploy it.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:22.120
I think you'll find
that, of course,

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.240
again, being in sales,
that it runs really well

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.170
on our platform.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:27.910
Because it's nice and
integrated and things like that.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:30.640
But it's designed to allow
you to get to that write once,

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.083
run anywhere mentality
that we were talking about,

00:05:33.083 --> 00:05:34.500
people like Urs
were talking about

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.562
and people like Thomas
Kurian were talking about.

00:05:36.562 --> 00:05:39.020
The other thing is it gives
you things like hermetic builds

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:42.810
in containers because it's
all container orchestration

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:44.400
and container automation.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.320
So you check that
right off your box.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.820
The other cool thing
that you've got

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.100
is built-in support for
things like canary deployments

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.240
and rollbacks.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.600
If something goes wrong, you
want that mean time to recovery

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.120
to be very, very short
very, very quick,

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:59.220
so you can get
things working again.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:00.400
I don't-- well.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.060
I do know how to do it
without using Kubernetes

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.440
and that entire stack.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.065
But it's painful, and I don't
like doing that sort of thing.

00:06:07.065 --> 00:06:09.440
Containers, on the other hand,
it's literally one command

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.050
and then you're all set.

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:12.750
And then last thing,
I like the fact

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.708
that you can get
service-level telemetry, which

00:06:14.708 --> 00:06:16.750
is a really cool component
that I'm going to talk

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.130
about a little bit later.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:19.860
Keep in mind, all
of this is designed

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.022
to keep developers
and operators happy.

00:06:22.022 --> 00:06:23.730
The reason why we're
putting these things

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:27.390
together is not just because
it's yet another new technology

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.265
that we think is an
interesting technology

00:06:29.265 --> 00:06:31.162
or we want you to do
it in a particular way.

00:06:31.162 --> 00:06:32.620
[BACKGROUND CHEERING AND
 APPLAUSE]

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:34.830
Kudos to the Anthos team for
getting a round of applause.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:37.080
But the reason why we're
putting these things together

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.790
is to make your
lives easier, to be

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.370
able to run those
services reliably

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:43.440
and without spending a
heck of a lot of time

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.200
trying to configure something.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:46.920
The tagline that
I always use when

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.020
I give presentations
is "infrastructure

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.320
for the sake of infrastructure
is pointless" in the sense of,

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.900
if I have to build up a
bunch of infrastructure

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:57.330
to run other
infrastructure, then

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:59.020
I have infrastructure
over here that

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.270
still needs to be managed
and configured and monitored

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:02.100
and so forth.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:03.990
The further you are
away from actually

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:05.580
running just an
application which

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.340
is tied to your business
goal, the less efficient

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.200
your overall system is.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:13.830
So with that in mind, let's
dive into the first bucket

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:15.240
that we have.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.460
This first bucket is
automation and toil.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.080
I want to start with that one.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:19.620
Why?

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.080
Well, predominantly
because automation and toil

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.230
are easy things to tackle,
especially in our platform.

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:28.590
So if we go back to
the diagram here this

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:33.390
is the overall diagram of
Anthos and some of the products

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:34.840
that are inside of it.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.680
So where would, say, automation
and toil-- if we really

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:40.560
want to focus on that, what
products would I recommend?

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.690
How would I recommend
using that sort of thing?

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:45.690
Well, it turns out it's
actually all of them.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.030
Because everything in a
cloud computing environment

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:49.530
is API driven.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.990
So anything that you
want to do is naturally

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:53.550
going to be scriptable.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:54.720
It's going to be callable.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:57.610
And it's going to be integrated
in the rest of the platform.

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:00.780
One of the nice benefits of
the promise of cloud computing

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:04.320
in general is that everything
that you have can just

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.990
be invoked with some sort of
API call, and then you're done.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.710
You don't have the equivalent
of an API call for a person

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:13.080
to have something
like remote hands.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.720
You don't have order
procurement purchasing, racking

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.310
and stacking, things like that.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:21.450
You can just hit it
and go from there.

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.610
However, the value
that you end up

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.440
getting from having an
entire system be automatable,

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:30.510
an entire system being
able to be easily scripted

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:35.100
is that you have this
API-centric design, right?

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.650
The concept of an
API-centric design

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:40.890
as opposed to a design that has
clients and other random things

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:42.960
is that we build the API first.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:44.460
And then we figure
out which clients

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:47.590
we want to build as a
result of that later on.

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:49.877
So your gcloud
command that you run

00:08:49.877 --> 00:08:51.960
or your console command
that you run-- if you ever

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.150
run that little command,
on the bottom it says,

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.440
view the equivalent JSON
post or the equivalent

00:08:56.440 --> 00:09:00.240
glcoud command to your clicks--
it's all the same stuff.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:03.390
I get a handful of customers
that always ask me like, well,

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.760
how do I make sure that a
person has access to this

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.485
but not access to that?

00:09:07.485 --> 00:09:09.360
And we always have to
remind them, well, it's

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.920
actually the API
call that you really

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:12.280
want to be focusing on here.

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:14.700
It's not necessarily restricting
access to just a command

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:16.660
line or just something else.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:18.170
So that's kind of important.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:20.490
But I want to focus a
little bit more on toil.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:23.880
To recap, toil-- this is the
SRE definition of toil that I

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:25.667
pulled right from the book.

00:09:25.667 --> 00:09:27.000
I'll kind of paraphrase it here.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.910
But, in general, you're
running a production service.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:31.740
You're doing work
to be able to enable

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:33.583
that particular
production service.

00:09:33.583 --> 00:09:35.250
It doesn't have a lot
of enduring value.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.610
And usually, the state of
the system is left the same.

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:40.980
My apologies to this
group of the audience

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.840
who's having difficulty
reading that sort of thing.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:44.340
I apologize.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:48.960
Anyway, to try to eliminate
toil, you have to ask yourself,

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.690
why am I doing this
particular task?

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.780
If I have to Bootstrap a
bunch of stuff even in a cloud

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:58.025
environment, can we go
set up the organization?

00:09:58.025 --> 00:10:00.400
Can we set up all of these
particular nodes, the firewall

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.800
rules, the monitoring,
and these sorts of things?

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.703
If you're doing that manually
repeatedly every time you

00:10:05.703 --> 00:10:07.370
want to deploy
something, every time you

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:08.870
want to get something
up and running

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:11.770
or recover from failure
or something like that,

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:13.430
you're spending a
lot of time doing

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:16.340
work that isn't
necessarily driving value

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:19.020
for your company or
your organization.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:23.150
So I want to focus on a couple
of the interesting products

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:24.893
that we've got in this area.

00:10:24.893 --> 00:10:26.310
If we look back
to the components,

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.140
we can orchestrate pretty
much all of this fun stuff.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:30.370
And how do we do that?

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:33.020
Well, big popular
things that we've got

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:36.500
are things like Cloud Deployment
Manager, which is basically

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:39.770
just a JSON-y
YAML-y Python-y type

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:41.750
thing that you defined
your infrastructure,

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:43.220
hit the button, and then boom.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:45.038
We take it and run it for you.

00:10:45.038 --> 00:10:46.580
You can do that same
thing with, say,

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:47.840
how she HashiCorp's Terraform.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:49.970
I think they have a booth
somewhere around here.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:51.360
We like them a lot.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:53.000
And we also like
them enough that we

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:56.300
include Terraform in the
Cloud Shell, for example.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:59.900
What I'm getting at here is
the left two big blocks here

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:01.915
are infrastructure as code.

00:11:01.915 --> 00:11:03.290
Again, we get back
to that notion

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:05.360
of a declarative
infrastructure where

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.220
you define how your
infrastructure is

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:08.530
going to be built once.

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:10.280
And then you let
something else figure out

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:13.610
how to move the state
to that desired state.

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:17.487
But I also want to mention we've
got Cloud Functions over here.

00:11:17.487 --> 00:11:19.070
We've got serverless
architecture that

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:22.790
allows you to have an event
driven workflow, meaning

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:25.130
if I want to talk
to Cloud Functions

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:27.320
or if I want to do
something else and key

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:29.630
off of some other
event that's happening,

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:33.020
Cloud Functions is a really good
solution to be able to do that.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:36.110
Speaking of which, here's
an interesting piece

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:37.880
of architecture
that I took right

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:40.610
from our cloud.google.com blog.

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:43.670
This is one of the
documented things

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:48.830
on how to do auto-inserts if
you upload a file into GCS.

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:50.690
So if you take a look here--

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:52.520
again, apologies to these guys.

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:56.420
You upload a file into
a cloud storage backend.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:59.990
You then figure out how to
move that into, say, BigQuery

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:02.420
to be able to do an analytics
and other fun stuff off

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:03.230
of that.

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:06.180
What's nifty about it
is it's a pipeline.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:08.030
So you've just
defined a pipeline

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:10.610
that you don't have to stand
up requisite infrastructure

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:12.860
to be able to listen for
these types of events.

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:15.560
It's included just
within Cloud Functions.

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:19.160
So if you're doing something and
it's-- let's say that task that

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:22.130
you need to do to continue
serving whatever the output

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:24.290
of this particular
pipeline would look like,

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:26.960
you can leverage Cloud Functions
to key off of those types

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:29.850
of event triggers, and then
take steps on your behalf.

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:31.850
The other thing which is
kind of cool about this

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:33.890
is how Cloud Functions
are just little,

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:36.720
tiny like JavaScript-y
wrapper-y things,

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:38.660
which means that you
don't necessarily

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:42.240
have to be a full SDK developer
to figure out what's going on.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:45.020
Usually it's just I
listen for an event,

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:47.990
I figure out some JSON, and
I post it to another backend,

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:50.780
and then it works well
together, again going back

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:54.630
to that API-centric design that
I was talking about before.

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.170
However, we've got some
other interesting tools

00:12:57.170 --> 00:12:58.880
that I want to talk about.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:01.190
One of the ones that I don't
think gets a lot of credit

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:03.450
these days is Cloud Composer.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:05.960
Cloud Composer is a
really powerful tool

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.420
that a lot of really
big enterprise companies

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:11.850
that I work with like
taking a look at.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:13.920
Well, you might say,
well, why is that?

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:15.800
What the heck is this
darn thing anyway?

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:18.020
Well, first of all, it's a
really cool Tetris piece.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:20.010
I mean, that's
pretty nifty there.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:22.700
But the other thing is
it's Apache Airflow.

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:25.880
So you might ask yourself,
OK, what's Apache Airflow?

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:27.880
Well, a bunch of DAGs.

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:29.420
OK, what's a DAG?

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:31.190
A Directed Acyclic Graph.

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:33.620
In other words, it's
an "if this/then that"

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:37.220
kind of thing, not the company
necessarily, but the principle

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:38.590
of implementation.

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:43.220
DAGs work in basically just a
standard flow chart type thing.

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:46.910
So you can program certain
specific tasks to be done.

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:50.720
Imagine if you wanted to have
a portal of some sort created

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:52.910
where you can provision
infrastructure based

00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:55.580
on the requests of your users.

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:59.122
And by users, I mean not
necessarily your end users,

00:13:59.122 --> 00:14:00.830
but corporate users,
something like that.

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:02.580
Like, hey, I need a
VM to go do something.

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:05.430
Or hey, I need this other
thing to be stood up.

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:07.280
Maybe it's a complex
series of steps which

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:09.260
actually requires invocation.

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:11.720
You might have written a
bunch of Terraform before.

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:13.460
But now, you're
constantly modifying it

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:15.330
as these new requests come up.

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:16.490
So what do you do?

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:20.070
Something like Cloud
Composer can do that for you.

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:22.400
Also what it can do, which
is kind of interesting,

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:25.100
is you can schedule
an invocation

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:28.100
or schedule an execution of
whatever you've programmed,

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.800
meaning that repeated tasks
that you might be doing,

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:34.070
hence something that's a really
great candidate for toil,

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:37.190
can now be automated away
and scheduled the way for you

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:39.170
to run repeatedly.

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:41.250
I'll give you a good
example of this.

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:43.700
This is automatic backups.

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:46.130
Wouldn't it be great if
leveraged the snapshotting

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:49.010
functionality that we have
inside of our standard GC

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:52.190
EVMs at a regular interval, then
you package it up and then ship

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:55.680
it and take it and have it
go do something as a result?

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:57.650
You can do this.

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.750
You don't have any manual
processes to do this.

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:02.420
And you don't really
have a lot of coding

00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:06.380
to be done because it has a very
easy-to-understand programming

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:08.660
language that invokes
all of these things.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:10.940
Likewise, you don't have
to allocate resources

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:12.085
for that to be run on.

00:15:12.085 --> 00:15:14.210
If you were to say, hey,
I've got a bunch of Python

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:15.740
to automate this,
I would go, cool,

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:17.110
where are you going to run it?

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:19.070
I've got to give you a server
to be able to run your Python.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:21.445
And then I've got to be able
to give it credentials to be

00:15:21.445 --> 00:15:22.725
able to make those API calls.

00:15:22.725 --> 00:15:24.350
Well, with Cloud
Composer, you can just

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:25.730
write it there,
click the button,

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:29.005
and then it goes and schedules
things, which is really handy.

00:15:29.005 --> 00:15:31.130
The other cool thing is it
integrates with the rest

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:33.395
of the Cloud based on its SDK.

00:15:33.395 --> 00:15:35.270
So if you want to automate
anything, not just

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:37.850
GC EVMs but literally
anything that we

00:15:37.850 --> 00:15:41.208
might have in the cloud,
that's a good way to do that.

00:15:41.208 --> 00:15:42.750
So if you wanted to
ask me like, hey,

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:44.930
how do we get started
with more automation,

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:48.050
reducing some toil in
my enterprise right now?

00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:51.440
I would say, infrastructure
as code solutions, Cloud

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:53.690
Functions, and Cloud Composer.

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:55.250
Take a look at all of them.

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:58.490
Figure out where it can be
used in your infrastructure.

00:15:58.490 --> 00:16:00.960
And then take it from there.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:02.855
Next up, we've got
release engineering.

00:16:02.855 --> 00:16:03.980
I love release engineering.

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:05.870
Because being that
DevOps, guy I do

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:07.802
a lot of DevOps-y type things.

00:16:07.802 --> 00:16:09.260
And one of those
things, of course,

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:11.390
is setting up CI/CD pipelines.

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:13.250
I always like to say CI/CD CA.

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:14.750
Because I think
it's important to be

00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:17.000
able to continuously audit
whatever you have already

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:19.310
built inside your
infrastructure to make sure

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:22.190
that you're in compliance with
whatever you wanted to build.

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:24.680
So if we go back to
the little chart here,

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:26.940
what piece am I
actually talking about?

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:29.390
It's that one right
there, Cloud Build.

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:31.640
I really like Cloud Build.

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:32.960
I really like it.

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:33.740
Why do I like it?

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:37.430
Well, first of all, I basically
just point it at a source repo.

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:39.920
And then I say, trigger
off of these tags.

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:42.440
And it goes and it builds
my containers for me.

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:44.240
Again, the requisite
infrastructure

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:46.580
we're talking about
is container native.

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:48.230
What's great about
that is, because

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:50.270
of the prevalence
of microservices--

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:53.810
and emphasis on "micro," like
we're not building six gig

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:56.600
containers here; we're
building small containers very

00:16:56.600 --> 00:17:00.170
frequently, integrating
things like unit tests into it

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:02.720
so that you have, say some
Go code, some Java code,

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:04.970
some Python, whatever
it happens to be--

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:07.575
it will build that, compile
it, run the unit tests,

00:17:07.575 --> 00:17:09.950
build the Docker container,
push the Docker container up,

00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:12.530
tag the Docker container,
and be ready to go.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:14.359
That's super powerful.

00:17:14.359 --> 00:17:17.390
Not only is that super powerful,
but because everything,

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:20.000
like I said, is API
driven and event specific,

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:21.873
you can then key off of that.

00:17:21.873 --> 00:17:23.540
So, for example, one
of the great pieces

00:17:23.540 --> 00:17:27.950
of solution architect developed
pipelines that we've got

00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:30.530
takes Cloud Build,
takes Spinnaker,

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:32.960
which is a great open
source continuous deployment

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:35.420
mechanism, and then
puts them together.

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:37.670
So Spinnaker watches
for new images

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:39.410
inside the Docker registry.

00:17:39.410 --> 00:17:41.760
That builds those images
and pushes them up,

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:43.430
which is pretty nice.

00:17:43.430 --> 00:17:46.940
But I want to focus
on the principles

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:49.100
that we're trying to
solve here, right?

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:51.890
So if we think about
release engineering,

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:54.890
here's a couple of different
choice words from the SRE

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:55.850
handbook--

00:17:55.850 --> 00:17:58.490
self-service, high
velocity, hermetic builds,

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:02.900
enforcement of releases in
a pragmatic, continuous way.

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:05.412
Back before Docker
became super popular,

00:18:05.412 --> 00:18:07.370
if you were to-- if I
were to put this slide up

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:10.070
to an audience full of
industry professionals,

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:12.330
you'd be like, hermetic builds?

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:13.970
What are you guys talking about?

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:16.610
Now that we have the concept
of containers widely available

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:19.370
and everyone agreed on them,
this is a heck of a lot

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:22.370
easier to get stood up.

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:25.127
I want to focus on two
other words that are there.

00:18:25.127 --> 00:18:26.710
While it might be
interesting or might

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:28.192
be tempting to
focus on continuous

00:18:28.192 --> 00:18:29.900
because you've got
continuous integration

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:31.640
and continuous
delivery, I actually

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:35.530
want to focus on hermetic.

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:39.030
And I want to focus
on enforcement.

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:41.570
These are interesting
developments here.

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:43.680
Number one, if you're
building a bunch of stuff

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:45.890
but your build environment
is constantly changing,

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:47.560
you're never building
the same thing.

00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:49.310
If you kick off a build
and you kick off a

00:18:49.310 --> 00:18:52.370
build of the exact same source
repo and the exact same thing,

00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:54.740
we should get the
exact same shasum

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:56.360
at the end of that build.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:59.390
There shouldn't be any weird
introduction back and forth.

00:18:59.390 --> 00:19:02.540
That is easier to accomplish
with things like microservices

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:05.250
and easier to accomplish
with things like containers.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:08.660
Or another word-- here's
some nice blue hexagons

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:10.010
to help you out with this.

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:11.480
You've got Container Builder.

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:13.470
And you've got the
Container Registry.

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:15.470
Again, you don't have to
worry about standing up

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:16.845
infrastructure to
be able to host

00:19:16.845 --> 00:19:19.498
all of these containers in a
secure way, in a private way

00:19:19.498 --> 00:19:22.040
with the appropriate amount of
identity and access management

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:23.150
on top of it.

00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:25.062
And you don't have
to worry about how

00:19:25.062 --> 00:19:26.270
to build all of these things.

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.270
Like, how to like
one that Go build?

00:19:28.270 --> 00:19:30.720
Or how to I one
that Java C command?

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:32.510
This will do that for you.

00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:35.120
The other cool
part about here, we

00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:37.220
can actually have
some enforcement stuff

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:40.270
taken care of, like I
mentioned, through Cloud IM.

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:42.232
And here's Spinnaker over here.

00:19:42.232 --> 00:19:43.940
So a lot of questions
I also get in terms

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:46.430
of setting up CI/CD
pipelines from customers

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:47.840
are, well, that's great.

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:50.690
I get the concept of
building this application

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:52.740
or this container,
something like that.

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:54.830
But how do I get
that for production?

00:19:54.830 --> 00:19:56.510
How do I make sure
that that's deployed

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:59.360
in a way that makes sense where
I have the appropriate change

00:19:59.360 --> 00:19:59.990
control?

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:01.155
How do I do that?

00:20:01.155 --> 00:20:03.530
Well, actually, Spinnaker
allows you to do that right out

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:04.460
of the box.

00:20:04.460 --> 00:20:05.900
As a matter of
fact, Spinnaker is

00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:08.840
natively aware of
things like Kubernetes

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:12.090
as a deployment target,
understands natively out

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:14.360
of the box things like GCP.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:17.270
So it understands how to work
with IMs, work with credentials

00:20:17.270 --> 00:20:19.890
and so forth to make
those things happen.

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:21.680
And not only that,
it also understands

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:25.250
how to do the appropriate
change mechanism so that you're

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:27.740
in compliance with whatever
regulated industry you

00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:29.510
happen to be in.

00:20:29.510 --> 00:20:31.970
Here's a really
cool architecture

00:20:31.970 --> 00:20:35.210
that our wonderful solution
architects have put together.

00:20:35.210 --> 00:20:38.450
So you've got Cloud Build,
Spinnaker equals happy.

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:39.350
I like that.

00:20:39.350 --> 00:20:41.210
It's CI/CD made easy.

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:44.450
Your developers essentially
get commit, get push.

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:44.960
That's it.

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:46.460
They don't have to
do anything else.

00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:49.160
It pushes out to a
Cloud Source repository.

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:51.500
Cloud Build picks it up,
figures out how to build it,

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:53.960
and tests it at the
unit test level,

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:55.650
builds the appropriate
Docker container,

00:20:55.650 --> 00:20:57.590
pushes the Docker container up.

00:20:57.590 --> 00:21:00.267
Spinnaker figures out that,
oh, you've got a new thing.

00:21:00.267 --> 00:21:01.850
Automatically, because
it's configured

00:21:01.850 --> 00:21:03.470
to do this in this
architecture--

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:05.990
automatically deploys
a Canary deployment,

00:21:05.990 --> 00:21:08.930
sends a certain amount of
traffic to that Canary,

00:21:08.930 --> 00:21:11.210
and then waits for you
to click the button

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:13.340
to update the percentage
of what you're

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:14.550
sending to the new version.

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:17.120
So let's say you're
doing a 10% 90% split.

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:18.740
It waits for you
to push the button.

00:21:18.740 --> 00:21:21.938
100% of the traffic rolls
over to the new version

00:21:21.938 --> 00:21:23.730
In other words, it kind
of looks like this.

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:26.490
This is the flow chart
of what that looks like.

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:28.790
So you change some
code, check it in.

00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:31.223
It detects the new tag,
builds the appropriate image,

00:21:31.223 --> 00:21:33.140
and then eventually hands
it over to Spinnaker

00:21:33.140 --> 00:21:34.310
that does that release.

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:36.230
All of this stuff, by
the way, for people

00:21:36.230 --> 00:21:39.818
that are taking pictures, is
available on cloud.google.com.

00:21:39.818 --> 00:21:41.860
These are literally just
screenshots that I took.

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:44.610
Or actually, I copied the
picture, which was kind of fun.

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:47.277
So if you want to head there, if
you actually just google things

00:21:47.277 --> 00:21:49.550
like "continuous
integration, Spinnaker,"

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:51.563
look for the first
cloud.google.com entry.

00:21:51.563 --> 00:21:53.480
And this actually walks
you through everything

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:56.122
you need to deploy along
with code samples, helm

00:21:56.122 --> 00:21:58.580
charts, and things that you
would need to actually get this

00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:01.520
stood up and working properly.

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:04.700
The important thing--
the all-important click

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:09.020
that managers and CEOs and other
types of executives want to see

00:22:09.020 --> 00:22:11.510
is the we didn't just
push this to production

00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:12.890
and broke everything.

00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:15.420
This little man
icon here, that's

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:17.810
the "I'm waiting for
a human to be involved

00:22:17.810 --> 00:22:19.560
before I make a decision."

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:22.470
So this is literally a
screenshot of Spinnaker.

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:23.930
Push to production, yes or no?

00:22:23.930 --> 00:22:25.030
Continue, stop?

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:26.930
You hit the Continue
button, that's deployed.

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:28.430
And then you're all set.

00:22:28.430 --> 00:22:30.780
Do you have to write all
of the additional glue code

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:32.780
in order to get this up
and running and working?

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:34.940
No, that's literally
what Spinnaker is.

00:22:34.940 --> 00:22:37.580
It works natively on the
platform, works really well

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:39.200
with things like Cloud Build.

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:42.200
So again, you can go from
zero to continuous integration

00:22:42.200 --> 00:22:45.380
and delivery pipeline that's
enforced based on the change

00:22:45.380 --> 00:22:48.050
management process that
you want without having

00:22:48.050 --> 00:22:50.630
to do a lot of extra
work, hence why

00:22:50.630 --> 00:22:54.380
I called the talk "Batteries
Included DevOps" and "Batteries

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:55.252
Included SRE."

00:22:55.252 --> 00:22:56.960
By the way, we have
a great YouTube video

00:22:56.960 --> 00:22:59.495
which talks about the difference
between DevOps and SRE.

00:22:59.495 --> 00:23:00.870
I love the way
they summed it up.

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:02.450
They've got really
cool shirts, which

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:04.430
is Class SRE implements DevOps.

00:23:04.430 --> 00:23:05.660
I really love that.

00:23:05.660 --> 00:23:08.400
As an object-oriented
guy, I kind of like that.

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:10.170
Anyhoo, the last one
I want to talk about,

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:13.520
which is the most important
one, is monitoring and SLOs.

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:14.780
This is the big one.

00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:16.820
Everything up until now,
you're probably going,

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:18.170
depending on your
familiarity with Cloud

00:23:18.170 --> 00:23:20.418
native architectures, you're
like, yeah, I get that.

00:23:20.418 --> 00:23:21.710
I get how to build a container.

00:23:21.710 --> 00:23:23.850
I'm sold on the whole
Kubernetes thing.

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:25.790
What have you done
for me lately?

00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:27.350
Well, if you
haven't taken a look

00:23:27.350 --> 00:23:29.810
at some of these really
awesome monitoring systems,

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:32.450
this is going to be
super fun for you.

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:34.650
If we go back to the
architecture diagram,

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:35.650
what am I talking about?

00:23:35.650 --> 00:23:38.380
I'm talking about this
bad boy down here--

00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:40.730
Stackdriver Service Monitoring.

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:42.430
Now, wouldn't it
be great if as I'm

00:23:42.430 --> 00:23:44.772
deploying all of these services
into production or even

00:23:44.772 --> 00:23:46.480
into a staging
environment or development

00:23:46.480 --> 00:23:49.120
environment, logging and
monitoring happened out

00:23:49.120 --> 00:23:51.850
of the box without having
additional configuration,

00:23:51.850 --> 00:23:55.270
without having to stand up my
own ELK stack or my EFK stack

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:56.690
or anything else?

00:23:56.690 --> 00:23:59.815
What if it just knew how
all of this stuff worked?

00:23:59.815 --> 00:24:01.690
That's what I going to
talk about right here.

00:24:01.690 --> 00:24:04.990
But I'm going to talk about some
of the advanced stuff as well.

00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:08.800
Stackdriver Service Monitoring
is a really fascinating brand

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:10.840
new thing that we rolled out.

00:24:10.840 --> 00:24:11.500
So what is it?

00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:14.545
Well, it's SRE monitoring
made very easy.

00:24:14.545 --> 00:24:16.420
Now, why do I say it's
SRE monitoring and not

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:18.100
just regular monitoring?

00:24:18.100 --> 00:24:19.840
Because it's per
service that you've

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:22.630
defined inside of Kubernetes.

00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:25.607
Because you have a series of
services that you've deployed

00:24:25.607 --> 00:24:28.190
and you've said these services
are going to talk to each other

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:32.022
and so forth,, you can get all
of this additional information.

00:24:32.022 --> 00:24:33.980
Now, it might be a little
bit hard to see here.

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:36.310
But the key thing
to take away is

00:24:36.310 --> 00:24:40.010
we have automatic SLO and
error budget calculations.

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:41.470
So if you're
thinking to yourself,

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:43.780
OK, I buy the whole
error budget thing.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:46.910
I buy the whole SLO,
SLI, SLA kind of thing.

00:24:46.910 --> 00:24:49.390
But how do I actually
go implement that?

00:24:49.390 --> 00:24:51.380
Don't tell me that I
have to do something.

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:52.870
Just do it for me.

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:55.450
Stackdriver literally
has done that for you.

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:57.767
Handful of clicks inside
of the UI, and it's

00:24:57.767 --> 00:24:59.350
actually telling
you, this graph right

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:01.600
here is actually your
error budget graph.

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:04.240
This graph down here is
your service level objective

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:08.520
compliance, meaning if this
graph is going really low

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:11.980
and it goes down below
that, you've blown your SLO.

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:13.930
Your error budget is
going to be crazy high.

00:25:13.930 --> 00:25:16.500
And then you can correlate
it with specific events.

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:19.330
So who broke production?

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:20.500
Figure out who that was.

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:23.560
Figure out what the thing was
that they were able to get away

00:25:23.560 --> 00:25:26.260
with, because we want to have
a blameless post-mortem type

00:25:26.260 --> 00:25:29.110
culture, and then figure
out how to automate that

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:31.330
from ever happening again.

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:36.140
This is a wonderful tool to be
able to help you out with that.

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:37.810
The other thing I
want to talk about,

00:25:37.810 --> 00:25:39.735
which some people use
Stackdriver service

00:25:39.735 --> 00:25:41.860
monitoring-- by the way,
a lot of the network stuff

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:44.570
that it grabs is all coming from
Istio, which is really cool.

00:25:44.570 --> 00:25:46.780
You can an actual
service topology graph.

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:50.050
But the next one I want
to talk about is Trace.

00:25:50.050 --> 00:25:52.030
Now, as we move to
distributed systems

00:25:52.030 --> 00:25:55.090
and we have more and more
microservices working,

00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:59.160
the hard problem becomes
why is the site slow.

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:01.760
Or why is something else slow?

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:04.240
It's not just a case
of one specific service

00:26:04.240 --> 00:26:06.310
that we can go, oh, there it is.

00:26:06.310 --> 00:26:09.940
A specific call to a front
end might invoke dozens

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:11.980
of microservices on the back.

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:15.050
If all but one of them
is having a problem--

00:26:15.050 --> 00:26:17.990
or excuse me, if all but one
of them is working properly,

00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:20.800
you won't necessarily know
which one of the microservices

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:22.270
is going to be at fault.

00:26:22.270 --> 00:26:24.600
You really want to know,
ah, it was that microservice

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:25.600
that's having a problem.

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:27.520
Because let's say it's
overloaded or something

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:28.480
like that.

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:31.600
You need a distributed
tracing framework.

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:33.340
We released a white
paper a few years ago

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:36.850
called "Dapper," which was our
solution to solving distributed

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:37.870
tracing.

00:26:37.870 --> 00:26:41.770
As a result of that, we ended
up releasing the white paper.

00:26:41.770 --> 00:26:43.780
And the open source
community picked it up.

00:26:43.780 --> 00:26:45.520
If you've heard
of Zipkin, that's

00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:48.645
the open source implementation
of our Dapper methodology.

00:26:48.645 --> 00:26:50.020
But there's a
couple other things

00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:53.230
that we can implement or
work with things, like Jaeger

00:26:53.230 --> 00:26:57.610
if anyone's heard of Jaeger or
Open Tracing, things like that.

00:26:57.610 --> 00:27:00.270
But if you want this to just
work out of the box, again,

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:03.790
that batteries included
SRE and DevOps practice,

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:05.560
this is already built in.

00:27:05.560 --> 00:27:08.270
You've got agent support
for popular languages.

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:10.290
So I think there's
over a half dozen.

00:27:10.290 --> 00:27:13.200
Basically, unless you're writing
COBOL or something like that,

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:14.950
we've got a language
that's going to work.

00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:17.920
It's going to be able to pick
up and integrate with your code

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:18.880
base.

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:21.140
And also, it works with
things like Zipkin Collector.

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:23.208
So if you've already
instrumented based off

00:27:23.208 --> 00:27:25.000
of Zipkin, we can pick
that up and give you

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.670
a wonderful dashboard that shows
you exactly what's going on

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:29.170
in your infrastructure.

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:31.737
What's great about that is
all of the different graphs

00:27:31.737 --> 00:27:33.820
that you would want for
all of the different calls

00:27:33.820 --> 00:27:36.280
that you have, regardless of
the number of microservices

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:40.390
that it's going to hit, is
served up in an easy to digest

00:27:40.390 --> 00:27:44.110
way which makes
debugging very easy.

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:46.540
Speaking of debugging,
This is the last thing

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:48.790
I want to talk about
in this area, which

00:27:48.790 --> 00:27:51.410
is Stackdriver Debugging.

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:54.220
Now, just out of curiosity,
how many people here,

00:27:54.220 --> 00:27:59.640
by show of hands, are
using Stackdriver Trace?

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:01.200
Oh, there's a handful of people.

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:02.200
There's three people.

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:04.033
How many people are
using Stackdriver Debug?

00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:09.043
Do you work at Google?

00:28:09.043 --> 00:28:10.960
So there's only like a
handful of people here.

00:28:10.960 --> 00:28:12.110
Now, that's really interesting.

00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:13.068
It's really surprising.

00:28:13.068 --> 00:28:15.980
Because this technology has
existed for quite a while.

00:28:15.980 --> 00:28:19.390
And this is how Google does this
type of debugging in real time

00:28:19.390 --> 00:28:20.800
when things go wrong.

00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:22.930
The number one thing
when an error happens

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:25.400
is you have to recover
as quickly as possible.

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:29.720
Do the RCA afterwards, but
get that recovery back online

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:31.348
so that your users are happy.

00:28:31.348 --> 00:28:32.890
You don't want to
be the company that

00:28:32.890 --> 00:28:35.110
is losing a million
dollars an hour

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:37.440
because something
isn't running properly.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:40.120
Stackdriver Debugging
allows you to do production

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:42.370
debugging very simply.

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:45.340
Again, agent support for
popular languages and SDK.

00:28:45.340 --> 00:28:47.440
So you can integrate
with all of this stuff.

00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:49.690
But essentially, you can
insert breakpoints and things

00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:53.920
like that and receive
instrumentation from something

00:28:53.920 --> 00:28:55.720
that you have deployed
in production.

00:28:55.720 --> 00:28:57.597
It works great with,
say, Kubernetes Engine.

00:28:57.597 --> 00:28:59.680
And it also works great I
believe, with App Engine

00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:01.310
as well, which is really cool.

00:29:01.310 --> 00:29:04.060
And here's the really cool
part, at least for me.

00:29:04.060 --> 00:29:06.400
It integrates with your
source control management,

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:09.580
meaning it can even
go, this line of code

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:11.490
is emitting this
particular breakpoint.

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:14.620
Here's the debug
information that you wanted.

00:29:14.620 --> 00:29:16.360
That's really powerful.

00:29:16.360 --> 00:29:19.817
Because you always are going
to run into a situation

00:29:19.817 --> 00:29:21.400
where something in
production is going

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:23.890
to die for some random reason.

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:26.380
And you want to collect
better information

00:29:26.380 --> 00:29:29.710
if that occurs and figure
out how to correct it.

00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:31.750
Development
environments oftentimes

00:29:31.750 --> 00:29:35.140
don't 100% match
that of production.

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:37.090
Obviously, DevOps best
practices that they

00:29:37.090 --> 00:29:38.620
should as much as possible.

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:41.050
But it's difficult to be able
to get the little fine-tuned

00:29:41.050 --> 00:29:43.180
details that you might
have, particularly

00:29:43.180 --> 00:29:44.890
if users are using
the site a little bit

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:47.080
differently than something else.

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:48.880
So Stackdriver Debugging
is super awesome

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:50.300
to be able to do that.

00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:53.020
So if you take a look at all
of these things, you can go,

00:29:53.020 --> 00:29:54.730
wow, I can have automation.

00:29:54.730 --> 00:29:56.530
I can start to eliminate toil.

00:29:56.530 --> 00:29:59.110
I can keep things simple because
we have specific tools built

00:29:59.110 --> 00:30:00.610
for specific things.

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:03.280
And the SRA book tells
us that tools and culture

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:05.387
are inextricably linked.

00:30:05.387 --> 00:30:07.720
And we've got all of these
additional tools out of a box

00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:10.780
to help with things like
advanced SLO calculation.

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:15.130
You can go from zero to
like production grade SRE

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:17.680
best practices with
very little modification

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:18.758
to what you're doing.

00:30:18.758 --> 00:30:20.800
You just pop it, take a
look at some of the stuff

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:24.140
that we've got in our platform
and take it from there.

00:30:24.140 --> 00:30:26.890
So last thing I
want to talk about,

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:30.190
which might be unique to our
platform as opposed to, say,

00:30:30.190 --> 00:30:32.350
other platforms and
whatnot, is that we've

00:30:32.350 --> 00:30:34.850
got help for if and
when you need it.

00:30:34.850 --> 00:30:36.640
This is not a
journey that I advise

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:38.410
people take by themselves.

00:30:38.410 --> 00:30:40.300
We refer to
customers as partners

00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:43.330
because it's a joint
partnership that we embark on.

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:46.660
If you're successful on our
platform, we're successful.

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:49.120
If we just go, hey, here's all
of our products, good luck,

00:30:49.120 --> 00:30:50.980
and then everything
fails for you,

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:52.370
you're not going to be happy.

00:30:52.370 --> 00:30:55.480
And we're not going to be happy
as a result. All of this stuff

00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:58.660
has to happen in the nature of
collaboration back and forth.

00:30:58.660 --> 00:31:01.180
So you've got an interesting
feature, work with your account

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:04.330
team to try to get that feature
pushed up into production.

00:31:04.330 --> 00:31:06.850
Meet with engineering
leadership to get their insight

00:31:06.850 --> 00:31:08.710
into what we see the future as.

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:10.210
But we want insight
from all of you

00:31:10.210 --> 00:31:12.100
as well because we
want to know what's

00:31:12.100 --> 00:31:13.840
interesting in your
industry, so we

00:31:13.840 --> 00:31:15.820
can incorporate that feedback.

00:31:15.820 --> 00:31:18.960
A couple of things specifically
with respect to SRE

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:20.830
and sort of DevOps-y
type things,

00:31:20.830 --> 00:31:23.380
we've got Customer
Reliability engineers

00:31:23.380 --> 00:31:26.140
and we also have
Professional Services.

00:31:26.140 --> 00:31:30.670
Is my friend Eric
here in the audience?

00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:34.120
Not that Eric, but thank you.

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:37.600
So my other friend
was actually a PSO.

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:39.850
He's a member of a Professional
Services Organization.

00:31:39.850 --> 00:31:43.060
And they're working
on a specific offering

00:31:43.060 --> 00:31:45.430
to help you out that's SOW
driven, or Statement of Work

00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:47.350
driven, to help evaluate
some of the stuff

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:49.870
that you're working on and
help you actually transition

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:51.343
applications up to production.

00:31:51.343 --> 00:31:54.010
But it's a little bit more hands
on, a little bit more detailed.

00:31:54.010 --> 00:31:57.190
The really cool thing is our
Customer Reliability engineers.

00:31:57.190 --> 00:32:01.030
These are like Google SREs
that help work in your company

00:32:01.030 --> 00:32:05.350
to take applications that you
have, do that SRE assessment,

00:32:05.350 --> 00:32:09.500
and then pull that into a modern
way of thinking about things.

00:32:09.500 --> 00:32:10.432
It's interesting.

00:32:10.432 --> 00:32:11.890
Part of the reason
it's interesting

00:32:11.890 --> 00:32:13.010
is because it's free.

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:15.010
But part of the reason
why it's also interesting

00:32:15.010 --> 00:32:17.290
is because it requires
a substantial investment

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:19.510
of your time to be
able to modernize

00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:21.430
that type of application
and actually get

00:32:21.430 --> 00:32:23.060
things working really well.

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:26.320
I highly recommend either one
of these types of services

00:32:26.320 --> 00:32:29.200
depending on what your mix is,
depending on what your fit is

00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:31.960
because we want to see
people start adopting

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:34.210
this type of better site
reliability engineering

00:32:34.210 --> 00:32:36.850
practices to leverage
our platforms,

00:32:36.850 --> 00:32:38.470
understanding
regionally and zonality

00:32:38.470 --> 00:32:40.300
and figuring out
how to make sure

00:32:40.300 --> 00:32:44.050
you get that fifth nine that
you're looking for by deploying

00:32:44.050 --> 00:32:45.910
really large scale
infrastructures that

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:48.130
have proper disaster
recovery, that

00:32:48.130 --> 00:32:51.140
adhere to all of the principles
that I was talking about today.

00:32:51.140 --> 00:32:52.300
So they're there.

00:32:52.300 --> 00:32:54.783
Now, I kind of went
through this very quickly.

00:32:54.783 --> 00:32:57.200
But I wanted to save some time
for some questions and what

00:32:57.200 --> 00:32:57.850
not.

00:32:57.850 --> 00:33:00.890
We've got some CRE people
that are here in case

00:33:00.890 --> 00:33:03.650
you've got questions
about CRE engagements

00:33:03.650 --> 00:33:06.120
or some best practices
and some other things.

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:09.170
And I think I've got, like,
what, 10 minutes, 15 minutes

00:33:09.170 --> 00:33:10.730
like that?

00:33:10.730 --> 00:33:12.130
SPEAKER: A good 15 minutes.

00:33:12.130 --> 00:33:13.380
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: 15 minutes?

00:33:13.380 --> 00:33:13.880
Awesome.

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:19.390
So with that, just to recap so
everyone's on the same page,

00:33:19.390 --> 00:33:22.590
this is what the Anthos
platform looks like.

00:33:22.590 --> 00:33:24.655
These are all of the
components inside of it.

00:33:24.655 --> 00:33:26.280
The big thing is that
you've got things

00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:27.900
like Kubernetes and Istio.

00:33:27.900 --> 00:33:29.395
You've got things like Knative.

00:33:29.395 --> 00:33:31.020
You've got something
that automatically

00:33:31.020 --> 00:33:32.580
builds the containers for you.

00:33:32.580 --> 00:33:34.500
You've got something
that monitors it.

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:38.320
And it runs on a platform in a
very easy and integrated way.

00:33:38.320 --> 00:33:40.980
In other words, if you wanted
to put up some blue hexagons

00:33:40.980 --> 00:33:43.080
and then match them with
the specific things,

00:33:43.080 --> 00:33:46.320
I would say you've got
Debugger for embracing risk.

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:50.040
You've got Stackdriver Service
Monitoring for your SLOs.

00:33:50.040 --> 00:33:52.200
You've got Composer to
help you out with something

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:55.050
like eliminating toil or
additional automation,

00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:57.360
Deployment Manager or
any other infrastructure

00:33:57.360 --> 00:34:00.080
as code type offering,
Stackdriver Trace

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:02.730
super important to try
to figure out as you move

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:04.590
towards these microservices.

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:08.130
You've got Cloud in Spinnaker
for your release engineering.

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:10.500
And then lastly,
you've got simplicity.

00:34:10.500 --> 00:34:14.520
So with this integrated
bundled products

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:16.650
that we have with
what we call Anthos

00:34:16.650 --> 00:34:19.679
that can run anywhere that has
all of these things integrated,

00:34:19.679 --> 00:34:22.620
it's very simple to go
from zero to whatever

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:25.199
it is that you want to be able
to deploy in production very

00:34:25.199 --> 00:34:26.520
quickly.

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:28.500
And with that, thanks a bunch.

